# Runtime item 4: cold rendering investigation and improvement plan

Implementation follow-up, 28 October: the [runtime changes and complete native proof](NATIVE_SHADER_PREPARATION_2026_10_28.md) fix callback scheduling and split Compact rear construction. All 192 programs import with matching context flags, but first-draw and memory gates still fail; native production integration remains deferred.

27 October 2026. Investigation and plan only. The best next prototype is **compiling exact Compatibility programs in a separate process, then importing their binaries in bounded steps**. Smaller GDScript batches cannot interrupt a blocking driver call. The desktop controls below also reject a compiler-thread setting and an ordinary shared-context worker as sufficient solutions. This is a promising direction, not a completed runtime fix or Android recommendation.

First make the small, independently justified correction: rename the asynchronous Garage warming helper `_draw()`. It currently also overrides the engine's CanvasItem draw callback.

The [machine-readable report](evidence/cold-render-investigation-2026-10-27/report.json) contains commands, source hashes, raw results and limitations. No effort estimates are included.

## What is established

The native Garage trace repeatedly enters the OpenGL driver while Godot is linking a shader program. Of 186 sampled thread stacks inside recorded frames above 200 ms, 175 have an NVIDIA driver/compiler module at the top. The most frequent nearest Godot return address is RVA `0x1411778`, immediately after the indirect `glLinkProgram` call. Disassembly of the exact executable and an in-process comparison of that function pointer with `wglGetProcAddress("glLinkProgram")` agree. A diagnostic trace of the actual call then records a 368.973 ms link and captures that program's generated GLSL. This attributes a real stall to a native call; it does not assign every loading delay to that call.

The pinned engine synchronously queries compile status after each stage and link status after linking. Its existing binary-cache loader also restores programs synchronously. See the [exact engine source](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/godotengine/godot/ed1daf0bf/drivers/gles3/shader_gles3.cpp). The Compatibility pipeline counters stay zero throughout these stalled draws, so those counters are unsuitable as proof of successful preparation here. Godot's modern pipeline-precompilation system [does not cover Compatibility](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/performance/pipeline_compilations.html).

The existing engine cache already works: the baseline profile contains 32 program-cache files totaling 30,785,415 bytes. Reusing that profile reduces preparation substantially, but retains 168 ms draw and 290 ms frame peaks. Enabling shader caching is therefore not a missing fix. Driver updates, new programs and a fresh application cache still need a first-use solution.

Both planar reflections use the original shared World3D. Hidden warm-up geometry is shared with those views; there is no evidence that separate, fully visible reflection-scene copies bypass the mesh batches. Existing program-feature grouping, round-robin batches, staged particles, retained particle proxies, shared tyre shader and Compact fitting reuse are credited as implemented.

