## Why the separate-process prototype leads

The driver exposes both parallel-compile extensions and already reports the maximum compiler-thread hint, `0xffffffff`. The [KHR extension](https://registry.khronos.org/OpenGL/extensions/KHR/KHR_parallel_shader_compile.txt) supplies a hint and completion queries; it does not promise that every driver call will fit a frame budget.

A small native harness uses the **exact captured game GLSL**, with a simpler standalone OpenGL state. Six corrected controls compare the ordinary compile/status sequence, deferred status queries, and a worker context sharing programs with a main context on a different window surface:

| Approach | Observed result, two runs |
| --- | --- |
| Ordinary sequence | Link-status call blocks 371–403 ms |
| Compile both stages and link, then poll completion before querying status | Status query still blocks 104–105 ms; completion polls themselves are below 0.8 ms |
| Compile in a separate shared context/thread, with the main context drawing | Main-context clear/swap call still blocks 321–340 ms; resulting shared program is valid |
| Compile in a separate process, with another process drawing | Compiler takes 397–456 ms; drawing process's largest GL call is 0.606–1.195 ms, versus 0.491 ms in its no-compiler control |
| Load the helper's program binary in a fresh process | `glProgramBinary` takes 2.678–2.896 ms; link status passes in 23–76 μs |

The independent drawing process completes 125–127 heartbeats in two seconds, versus 126 in the control. Its maximum full loop interval remains about 31 ms, including the Windows sleep/scheduling interval. This is **not a 60 FPS or touch-latency result**. The process test renders only clear/swap frames, does not exercise the imported program in a real Garage draw, and samples one shader. Actual engine linking and standalone linking can block in different API calls because their GL state and setup differ.

The result supports testing process isolation before investing in a full shared-context compiler. It does not prove portable program binaries or removal of later state-dependent driver compilation. The [program-binary specification](https://registry.khronos.org/OpenGL/extensions/ARB/ARB_get_program_binary.txt) requires handling configuration-dependent rejection, restoring uniforms and possible further state-dependent work. No cache binary is shared between different devices or shipped as a universal asset.

## Concrete implementation plan, in order

1. **Remove the accidental callback.** In `game/ui/garage_preloaded_studio.gd`, rename `_draw()` to `_warm_frame()` and update both explicit callers. Require construction completion and the existing permission gate before submitting a warm frame. Keep engine drawing separate from the warming state machine. Verify that construction, resize, thumbnail changes and cancellation never schedule warming implicitly, and that explicit warming still restores all held visibility/culling and the original live world. The tested rename is diagnostic only; this runtime edit remains to be made.

2. **Prove the complete native path before choosing a production architecture.** Extend the one-program harness to actual Garage UI rendering, all native shader families used by its selected car, and the first draw after binary import. Include GL context creation, source generation, uniform/UBO setup and real vertex layouts in the trace. Time `glCompileShader`, compile/link queries, `glLinkProgram`, `glProgramBinary`, uniform setup, uploads and the first actual draw separately. The process design advances only if the main UI remains responsive, imported programs draw correctly, and helper memory is acceptable. On Android, test the equivalent private service process with a native EGL/GLES context, not a desktop executable or shared-context assumption. Android supports components in [separate manifest-selected processes](https://developer.android.com/guide/components/processes-and-threads), but driver behavior and lifecycle still need device evidence. This device prototype is future work and was not run here.

3. **Add a supported engine preparation API.** Work in the pinned engine's `drivers/gles3/shader_gles3.{h,cpp}` around `_build_variant_code`, `_compile_specialization`, `_load_from_cache` and `_get_uniform_locations`. Produce immutable jobs containing exact generated stages, variant/specialization defines, pre-link attribute/output bindings and transform-feedback configuration. Deduplicate by the engine's source/variant identity plus relevant link state. Return `queued / preparing / binary ready / installed / failed`; rendering must not synchronously wait for queued work. Keep this change in an engine source patch and reproducible build. The investigation's executable-address hook must never become a runtime integration. Record the new engine identity and verify canonical replay parity through the normal compatibility process.

4. **Implement one bounded compiler helper and binary handoff.** Use one helper process, one active program and a deduplicated queue initially capped at 32 jobs / 16 MiB of aggregate source-and-result payload. Run compilation on that process's worker thread. Return binaries through local IPC/private temporary files with a versioned header, exact source/link key, length, binary format and checksum. Validate against the current GL implementation and the existing engine cache identity; publish through atomic cache writes. Treat helper death, context loss, zero binary formats, corrupt output and driver changes as recoverable misses. Initial payload bounds are proposed controls, not measured memory consumption; include the helper's private/graphics memory in the whole application's optional-work budget. Keep it disabled if the existing budget cannot accommodate it.

5. **Install one program at a time and measure the indivisible calls.** Integrate results with the existing engine cache instead of replacing its format with an unrelated blob. Move file reads, validation and decompression off the render thread; install at most one program per allowed frame with a proposed 4 ms soft preparation budget. Run the original uniform, texture-unit and uniform-block setup after binary load. Publish readiness only after these operations succeed. A single import or first real draw can exceed that budget; detect it and fail the prototype's responsiveness gate rather than claiming the scheduler can interrupt it. Reusing a valid prior program is permitted only for the same exact program identity. Never substitute a simpler material to hide a pending program.

6. **Connect exact program readiness to Garage and race readiness.** `garage_preloader.gd` supplies the existing idle, input, navigation, account, foreground and pressure gates. `garage_preloaded_studio.gd` requests the programs before first native use, then verifies actual draws for the live viewport, both original planar reflections, the exact carousel capture and restoration to live settings. Derive settings from the actual views, including their different MSAA/shadow modes. Keep an early Garage request navigable while its exact live view prepares. Preserve saved paint, parts, wear, orbit, same-world adoption and stale-selection/account fences. For racing, connect `game.gd`, `world_render_preparation.gd` and `effect_render_preparation.gd` to the same preparation service; retain the staged/pooled effects already implemented. Require first real emissions, weather and lighting contexts before removing the existing race loading screen. Preparation must leave canonical state, tick zero, countdown and recording untouched. Optional jobs pause or retire on race entry, backgrounding or pressure; admitted native work may finish in the helper without publishing stale results.

7. **Attribute and remove the remaining race-frame outlier.** The fresh actual-App race profile passes 60 checks across 12 loads, but the first Star load takes 44.417 s with a 4.980 s peak frame; first Freight takes 16.038 s with a 1.882 s peak. The label `cache` remains set across other loading operations and is not a cause. Add bounded per-load UTC spans around world-cache access, car creation/resource load, material/texture upload and native preparation, with driver-module refresh in the sampler. Capture the exact offending frame before changing another subsystem. Existing world packing is only 3.101 ms and warm instantiation 0.634 ms in this profile; optimizing those cannot explain away the outlier. The first car's largest cooperative slice is still 49.487 ms: split that identified detail/resource work after native attribution confirms its own cost. Avoid repeating the already completed trail/proxy/grid fixes.

8. **Gate completion on cold input, frame time, quality and memory.** Use a reproducible source/build snapshot and fresh app caches, repeat caches, invalid/old caches, driver identity changes, all seven cars and all themes. Exercise immediate entry, active touches, cancellation, account/car replacement, background/foreground, pressure eviction and helper failure. For a 60 Hz target, proposed acceptance is p99 interactive frame time ≤16.7 ms, no preparation-caused frame above 33.3 ms, and deliberate-input dispatch ≤50 ms; report raw worst cases and misses. Verify original rendered images/reflections/carousel and first real effects, canonical replay parity, and the combined main/helper PSS plus graphics-memory plateau. These are targets, not achieved results. If target Android drivers reject binaries, serialize the live context, defer work to first draw, or exceed memory, retain the experiment and reassess the renderer path; do not ship the desktop result as an Android solution.

## Current race result and retained limits

Repeated race loads now peak at **26–38 ms**, with 5.4–6.7 ms prepared draw waits, compared with the earlier 208–246 ms observations. The intervening loading fixes deserve credit; these are not paired speedup measurements. First new-track loads after Star/Freight still peak at 86–141 ms. Cold rendering and some non-render preparation therefore remain open even though the repeated bulk-particle defect has improved.

Seven Garage scripts are reloaded from frozen task-start sources in each owned diagnostic process. Twelve relevant source snapshots and per-run live hashes are retained. Concurrent App changes are recorded; the race probe uses the actual shared tree. This is not an immutable whole-tree or final release run. The original warm phase's 2x shadowless carousel settings are preserved as tested; subsequent broader capture behavior must be derived from the final implementation rather than copied from an old report.

Retained findings: the first App fixture reads `carousel_texture` after normal App transfer consumes it; its one failed assertion remains recorded and the repeat checks ownership before transfer. The first shader-capture DLL lacks `libwinpthread-1.dll` and fails two checks; a statically linked repeat passes all ten. The Mobile control completes its 14 assertions and captures but does not finish teardown; the runner terminates that owned child after 240 seconds. This is not clean renderer acceptance. The first shared-context harness uses one surface and is superseded by the separate-surface controls. An initial unisolated API-inspection attempt cannot rotate a sandboxed log; the isolated repeat exits normally. The PowerShell pixel helper initially encounters the local script execution policy; running that authored helper with process-local `-ExecutionPolicy Bypass` succeeds without changing machine policy. Native logs retain the sandbox root-certificate error. Separate-render-thread logs contain driver/engine warnings and remain diagnostic controls. Native sampling suspends only owned child threads, with a maximum recorded 10.309 ms suspension in the Garage run; timings from sampled cases are not comparative benchmarks. Later-loaded modules are unresolved in parts of the parallel-hint/race traces, and the bounded race UTC timeline does not cover every load; those traces do not establish native attribution of the 4.980 s frame.

No production runtime source was changed by this investigation. No physical-device attachment query/test, APK/AAB, real authentication/purchase, owner-save/live service mutation, deployment, owner-process restart or main-index staging/commit/merge was performed. The next deliverable is the complete native-path prototype and its go/no-go evidence, followed by integration only if those gates pass.
